When a regular function is passed to `setInterval`, it receives its own `this` binding, which doesn't refer to the `Timer` instance. Instead, in non-strict mode, `this` defaults to the global object, and in strict mode or module mode, it becomes `undefined`. As a result, `this.seconds` evaluates to `undefined`, and adding 1 to `undefined` produces `NaN`.

The solution is to replace the regular function with an arrow function, since arrow functions capture `this` from their surrounding `start()` method rather than having their own binding:

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

When a regular function is passed to `setInterval`, that function has its own `this` context, which isn't bound to the `Timer` instance. In non-strict mode, `this` defaults to the global object; in strict mode or module code, `this` is `undefined`. This causes `this.seconds` to be `undefined`, and since `undefined + 1` evaluates to `NaN`, the counter breaks.

The solution is to use an arrow function instead, because arrow functions don't have their own `this` — they inherit `this` from their enclosing scope, in this case the `start()` method:

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

